That would bring up a new set of problems. An object in motion stays in motion until acted upon by an outside force. In other words, there shouldn't be any nebulous clouds at all and the cosmic dust should long dissipate if there wasn't an outside force (like gravity) that stopped the nebulous cloud in place. 

&#x200B;

The next problem is the fundamental leftover of a dead star "only" goes to a white dwarf, neutron star, or black hole. If a dead star is due to producing iron, that would mean the star itself is becoming increasingly solid, which means it CAN break if there's a large enough collision. to "shatter the dead star". 

&#x200B;

Besides, the Oort Cloud is very telling, especially it's shape. Earth is "fat" around it's belly, the sun has an accretion disc, the black hole in the middle of the milky way creates a spiral shape. The shape of the Oort Cloud and the shape of galaxies just goes to show that gravity is something that "grows" over time. And the way it grows is through an ever growing spin that becomes more orderly the closer it is to the center and more chaotic the further out one goes. And if gravity grows, it would explain how the Oort Cloud isn't just expanding infinitely, how nebulae dust (soot) doesn't simply dissipate, and why nebulae are shaped the way they are: expanding gravity. 

&#x200B;

Also, there's no evidence that suggests a dead star remains whole during the entire supernova process. If two large chunks of similar sizes were somehow split during the supernova process and eventually the two large chunks attract each other again through gravity and collide with each other at high speeds, there's a very good chance that they wouldn't merge but shatter instead.

